 # Perlin Noise 2D
 
-Also called: 2D Perlin Noise, Perlin Noise (Improved), Perlin's noise function, Noise (Perlin)
+Also called: 2D Perlin Noise, Classic Noise, Gradient Noise, Noise (Perlin), Perlin Noise (Improved), Perlin Noise Generation, Perlin's noise function
 
 ## Overview
-Perlin Noise 2D is a lattice gradient noise function that generates smooth, pseudo-random scalar values by interpolating dot products of gradient vectors and distance vectors at the corners of the integer lattice cell containing the query point [S3][S4]. It solves the problem of creating continuous, coherent procedural textures for volumetric data (such as clouds, terrain, or smoke) without the repeating patterns of simple value noise [S1, unofficial][S7, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its sibling [Simplex Noise](978-simplex-noise.md) by using a square grid topology and cubic/quintic interpolation, whereas Simplex uses a triangular grid to reduce directional artifacts [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from [Improved Perlin Noise (4D)](3723-improved-perlin-noise-4d.md) by operating in two dimensions, resulting in lower computational cost but potentially higher grid-oriented artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+Perlin Noise 2D is a lattice gradient noise function that generates smooth, pseudo-random scalar values by interpolating dot products of gradient vectors and distance vectors at the corners of the integer lattice cell containing the query point [S1][S10, unofficial]. It solves the problem of creating continuous, coherent procedural textures for volumetric data (such as clouds, terrain, or smoke) without the repeating patterns of simple value noise [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its sibling [Simplex Noise](978-simplex-noise.md) by using a square grid topology and quintic interpolation, whereas Simplex uses a triangular grid to reduce directional artifacts [S8, unofficial]. It is distinguished from [Improved Perlin Noise (4D)](3723-improved-perlin-noise-4d.md) by operating in two dimensions, resulting in lower computational cost but potentially higher grid-oriented artifacts [S1].
 
